    \u043a\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u0443\u044e \u0442\u043e\u0442 \u0441\u0430\u043c \u0438
    \u0437\u0430\u0432\u0435\u0449\u0430\u043b."
  interestingFactsEn: "The life and death of Archimedes are shrouded in many legends, anecdotes and amazing stories that have been passed down from generation to generation. Here are the most interesting and entertaining facts that paint him not just as a dry scientist, but as a person with a bright character and incredible intuition. 1. \"Eureka!\" and the bath (the legend of the crown) This is perhaps the most famous scientific joke in history. King Hiero suspected the jeweler of stealing gold when making a ritual crown and asked Archimedes to expose the deception without destroying the product. Archimedes agonized over the problem for a long time, until one day, plunging into a bathtub filled to the brim, he noticed that some of the water had splashed out. At that moment it dawned on him: the volume of displaced water was equal to the volume of his body. This means that in order to check the crown, you need to compare the volumes of displaced water for the crown and for a gold bar of the same weight. Legend has it that he was so excited that he jumped out of the bath and ran naked through the streets of Syracuse, shouting at the top of his voice, “Eureka! Eureka!\" (“Found! Found!”). The experiment confirmed the substitution: the jeweler was executed, and humanity received the law of hydrostatics. 2. “Give me a fulcrum, and I will change the world.” Archimedes not only discovered the law of leverage, but also clearly demonstrated its capabilities. To impress King Hiero, he allegedly declared that with the help of a lever and pulleys he could move any load, no matter how heavy it was. The king ordered this to be demonstrated on the ship Syracuse, a huge loaded trireme that hundreds of people could not budge. Archimedes installed a system of complex blocks (pulley hoists) and, sitting at some distance, calmly pulled the rope. The ship floated smoothly through the water, as if pulled by an invisible force. Since then, the phrase “Give me a foothold...” has become a symbol of the power of the mind, triumphing over brute physical power. 3. Celestial sphere - the ancient computer Archimedes designed an amazing mechanical planetarium - the “celestial sphere”. It was a complex device with a system of gears that showed the movement of the Sun, Moon and five then known planets across the sky. Surprisingly, this mechanism could predict the phases of the moon and even lunar eclipses. This invention so amazed his contemporaries that even several centuries later the famous orator Cicero wrote admiringly about it. In fact, it was the world's oldest analog computer, two thousand years ahead of its time. 4. Defense of Syracuse against Rome When Roman troops besieged Syracuse, the elderly Archimedes turned his hometown into an impregnable fortress. “Claw of Archimedes”: He designed cranes that lowered onto enemy ships, grabbed them by the bow with iron hooks, lifted them into the air, and then dropped them down, smashing them against the rocks. Witnesses said that the ships capsized and sank, and the crews were terrified.Giant catapults: He created throwing machines of different power: some hit over long distances, hitting ships before they reached the shore, others - at close range, breaking through the sides. As a result, the Roman commander Marcellus, known for his bravery, exclaimed in horror that Archimedes was waging war on him, using ships as “water scoops,” and compared the scientist with the hundred-headed mythical giant Briareus. The Romans moved from an assault to a long siege, accepting that they feared one old man more than an entire army. 5. The mystery of “fire mirrors” There is a legend that Archimedes burned Roman ships, directing the sun's rays at them using a system of huge concave mirrors. While some historians consider this to be fiction (glass technology at the time was imperfect), modern enthusiasts and television shows (such as a series of experiments from the US Department of Defense) have proven that with a large amount of polished copper shields and clear weather, a wooden ship can actually be set on fire. So Archimedes could use this optical trick, although not as effectively as the legends describe. 6. Final: “Don’t touch my drawings!” The death of Archimedes in 212 BCE became a symbol of the death of science at the hands of barbarism. When the Romans entered the city during a night assault, the soldiers were ordered to find the scientist and bring him to Marcellus (who wanted to spare his life). According to the most famous version, 75-year-old Archimedes sat on the sand and drew geometric figures, completely immersed in solving the problem. When a Roman legionnaire approached him, demanding to follow him, the elder did not pay attention to him, but only waved his hand and said: “Don’t touch my drawings.” Enraged by the rudeness of the “prisoner,” the soldier hacked him to death with a sword right in the sand. They also say that the commander Marcellus was furious at such a murder, ordered the murderer to be executed and Archimedes buried with honors. 7. Last Will Archimedes valued his mathematical discoveries above engineering inventions. He bequeathed to carve on his tombstone a drawing of a ball inscribed in a cylinder and the proportion 2:3, since he considered the proof of the ratio of their volumes to be his most outstanding achievement. The irony of fate: 137 years later, the famous orator Cicero, being an official in Sicily, decided to find the tomb of the great scientist. Local residents knew nothing about her. Cicero wandered through the overgrown cemeteries until he noticed a stone with a cylinder with a ball peeking out from under the blackberries and nettles. It was the forgotten tomb of Archimedes, found precisely thanks to the geometric figure that he himself bequeathed."
